art saving and investing with  acorns and you can pause the screen or  look at the description to check out my  disclaimer including a trigger warning  to those with current or previous  experiences with disordered eating so as  always feel free to skip this video if  it is not supportive to your journey and  if you are not already subscribed hit  that subscribe button and follow me over  on tick tock and instagram abby's  kitchen also a big thank you to my  colleagues alita finney and janice baker  for their help and expertise on this  video this is a very big topic but i'm  going to try to keep it as simple as i  can so pre-diabetes occurs when blood  sugar levels are higher than normal but  not high enough to warrant a diabetes  diagnosis this suggests that our cells  are starting to become resistant to  insulin which causes glucose to build up  in the bloodstream instead of being used  for energy there is a lot of ground to  cover here so let's unpack some of the  big myths and tips for managing  pre-diabetes myth one having  pre-diabetes means it's just a matter of  time before you have diabetes so this is  a valid concern as five to ten percent  of people with pre-diabetes will develop  type 2 diabetes every year but even  though you are technically at an  increased risk the good news here is  that this trajectory can be reversed or  delayed through lifestyle changes we  even have long-term data suggesting that  lifestyle interventions like physical  activity and diet can decrease  pre-diabetes progression for as long as  10 years ultimately the goal here is to  reverse insulin resistance by making our  cells more insulin sensitive in other  words we want to make our cells more  responsive to insulin so that they can  efficiently absorb glucose from the  bloodstream and then lower the sugar in  our blood one of the best ways to  increase insulin sensitivity is through  regular exercise in fact one review of  the literature confirmed that regular  physical activity can help to improve  blood sugar levels and slow down the  progression of diabetes this is because  physical activity drives glucose into  the muscle which helps to boost insulin  sensitivity anywhere from 2 to 48 hours  post-workout so over time regular  physical activity can boost our insulin  sensitivity long-term thereby helping to  ward off diabetes progression not only  that but any reduction in visceral  abdominal fat specifically has also been  shown to improve insulin sensitivity so  exercise may be able to play an indirect  role here as well however it's very  important to note that visceral fat is  independent of body size so in other  words you can have a larger body and be  physically lean with little abdominal  fat or be in a smaller body and have  higher amounts of abdominal fat which  can ultimately impact insulin resistance  and this speaks to why bmi is not such a  great measure of health but we already  knew that obviously now in terms of  exercises that are most effective at  boosting insulin sensitivity one  meta-analysis found that aerobic  exercise and resistance training had the  most significant effect so for example  one study on non-diabetic participants  found that 60 minutes of cycling  increased insulin sensitivity for 48  hours post-workout another study on men  with insulin resistance found that  strength training over a three-month  period also improved insulin sensitivity  now of course we want to think about  small manageable changes that you can  sustain long term rather than like  jumping into this like six day a week  program when maybe you've never  exercised before in your life  [Music]  so this might look like going for a walk  or biking to work a few days a week  instead of driving uh maybe taking the  stairs instead of the elevator doing  some bodyweight exercises or  experimenting with weights or resistance  bands and don't forget that daily  activities like cleaning gardening or a  brisk walk also count myth number two  you need to cut carbs now when we eat  carbs it breaks down to sugar in the  body aka glucose which naturally raises  our blood sugar levels now for someone  who isn't insulin resistant the cells  are able to respond to the boost in  insulin let the sugar into the cells for  energy and then return blood sugar to  normal levels but in someone with  pre-diabetes who is becoming insulin  resistant the cells aren't as responsive  to insulin and therefore the blood sugar  levels remain higher than normal so if  you're trying to get your blood sugar  levels under control i can definitely  understand the knee-jerk reaction to  want to cut out all the carbs however  when we look at the larger body of  research low-carb diets have not  actually been shown to significantly  improve blood sugar levels compared to  normal or higher carb diets in fact  certain carbs may actually be highly  beneficial in blood sugar management as  a source of fiber and research has  consistently shown that soluble fiber  specifically can slow down glucose  absorption and improve post meal blood  sugar levels in fact one study found  that those who ate more soluble fiber  had lower levels of insulin resistance  another interesting factoid is that  soluble fiber helps to feed good gut  bacteria which can also help to lower  our insulin resistance not only that but  soluble fiber from foods like oats  barley and psyllium have been shown to  help lower cholesterol levels which is  another risk factor for developing  diabetes there are even diabetes  prevention studies showing that high  fiber diets combined with moderate  intensity physical activity help to  reduce the risk of diabetes by a  whopping 58  so the goal is to reverse diabetes  progression we definitely don't want to  sleep on those high fiber carbs the  ideal strategy here would be to obtain  fiber from a combination of different  types and sources of carbs including  fruits vegetables whole grains nuts  seeds and legumes and aim for 25 to 38  grams of fiber a day with that said it's  also important to pay attention to carb  quality as not all carbs are made equal  and some carbs will have a drastically  different effect on blood sugar levels  than others this is what we call the  glycemic effect of food otherwise known  as the glycemic index some carbs have a  higher glycemic index like white bread  meaning they will raise blood sugar  levels quicker whereas lower gi carbs  will help to slow down the release of  sugar into the bloodstream research has  even shown that replacing high gi foods  with low gi foods has shown clinically  significant improvements in blood sugar  levels for folks with diabetes so while  we definitely want to be prioritizing  high fiber low gi carbs it's also worth  noting that carbs are not usually eaten  in isolation but rather in combination  with other foods so even if a food has a  high glycemic index like white rice for  example if we combine this with a source  of protein fat and fiber aka make it a  hunger crushing combo it can help to  mediate the blood sugar response so for  example one study found that creating a  mixed meal of rice combined with bean  sprouts for fiber egg for protein and  oil for fat significantly lowered the  blood sugar response compared to just  eating the rice alone so this is all  just to say that we do not need to cut  carbs to prevent diabetes or even  sacrifice our favorite carbs if they  happen to fall higher on the glycemic  index instead we would want to focus on  carbohydrate quality by prioritizing  higher fiber lower glycemic index carbs  and or ensuring that we are dressing up  those carbs with a source of protein fat  and fiber to help slow down the blood  sugar response and now that we are on to  the topic of carbs let's discuss another  common diabetes myth you need to go  keto given the rise and popularity of  the keto diet many have jumped on the  keto bandwagon as a way to help manage  their blood sugar levels by drastically  reducing carb intake on top of that  because weight loss is a common  recommendation for those at risk of  diabetes many have turned to weight loss  diets like keto as a quick fix so even  though we know there's tons of research  support in the consumption of high fiber  carbs for the prevention and management  of diabetes is the keto diet necessary  or even safe have no fear safety is here  well technically the keto diet may help  to improve blood sugar levels given that  carbs are being drastically reduced  thereby lowering the needs for insulin  in the first place in fact one study  found that the keto diet helped to  improve blood sugar levels and  medication reduction in folks with  diabetes however what we aren't so sure  of is whether or not keto benefits or  further destroys insulin sensitivity  it's kind of a mixed bag of research in  this space most of the research  suggesting that keto may improve insulin  sensitivity suggests that it's likely  only a temporary improvement and not  permanent for example one study saw  improvements at six months but this was  not maintained after a year on top of  that we also know that higher intakes of  dietary fat may increase the risk of  elevated cholesterol levels and other  cardiovascular risk factors which again  we know increases the risk of diabetes  but of course this largely depends on  the type of fat as diets high in healthy  unsaturated fats don't appear to have  the same effects we also have research  showing that keto diets high in animal  proteins and fats are associated with a  two-fold increase in type 2 diabetes  whereas a keto diet high in plant-based  protein and fats was associated with a  decreased risk so again it really comes  down to diet quality of the foods  included in the diet not necessarily  the absence of carbohydrates so with all  that said do you absolutely need to go  on a keto diet to get your blood sugar  levels under control  no  especially if it's not something that  you think you could maintain long term  in fact the diabetes clinical practice  guidelines consistently recommends a  diet containing high fiber carbs for the  management of diabetes like the  mediterranean diet for example but can  the keto diet benefit some folks yeah of  course it might but again we don't have  enough long-term research to recommend  this across the board especially because  we know that the keto diet has very low  adherence and weight cycling or yo-yo  dieting also puts one at greater risk  for metabolic syndrome and diabetes so  if you are considering the keto diet to  miniature blood sugars it's really  important to weigh the risks and the  benefits and to work closely with a  registered dietitian all right moving on  to myth number four eating sugar causes  diabetes this is among the most common  myths that i hear when it comes to  diabetes and pre-diabetes so before you  decide to forgo the sugar in your coffee  i want to start out by saying diabetes  is a highly complex disease and there  are multiple factors that can increase  your risk in fact many risk factors for  diabetes aren't even within our control  family history of diabetes is a very  strong predictor of diabetes and  pre-diabetes so one study found that  family history increased the risk of  pre-diabetes by 26  older age and a history of gestational  diabetes are also major factors as for  the risk factors that are within our  control diet of course does play an  important role but so does physical  inactivity and smoking in other words if  your dad has diabetes you smoke every  day live a sedentary lifestyle don't  manage your stress and have high  cholesterol levels cutting out sugar  from your diet likely isn't going to  make or break your case first of all  smoking's bad you shouldn't smoke so  while we still want to be mindful of  added sugars and prioritizing higher  fiber carbs it's really important to  take a holistic approach to diabetes  prevention by focusing on all of the  lifestyle factors rather than just  zeroing in on the amount of sugar in  your tea next is myth number five you  need to lose weight to lower your risk  of diabetes so weight loss is among the  top recommendations for folks with  pre-diabetes or diabetes with most  research pointing to the benefits of  weight loss and diabetes management and  as i mentioned earlier this is because  visceral abdominal fat increases insulin  resistance but this association is  largely specific to visceral fat and not  necessarily other types of body fat or  just having a larger body in general and  like i said you can be in a larger body  or be considered obese by bmi standards  but actually be more lean and  metabolically healthy than someone of  normal bmi and vice versa so the  recommendation to lose weight across the  board to reduce your risk of diabetes is  a bit more nuanced than absolute and  when i consulted my dietitian colleagues  and diabetes experts alita finney and  janice baker they both agreed that  focusing exclusively on weight loss is  not the best approach weight loss is not  a behavior it's a side effect and while  we can't control all of the risk factors  for diabetes we really should be zoning  in on the behaviors that we actually can  control plus if obsessing over weight  loss is causing stress and anxiety  around food and your body this can  actually increase blood sugar levels  thanks to the role of our stress hormone  cortisol dieting ultimately is a  physical and emotional stressor on the  body and chronic stress has been shown  to make our cells more insulin resistant  over time so given that weight loss is  not something that we actively have  control over janus and elita both agreed  we should be focusing on actionable  behaviors like physical activity eating  habits smoking cessation sleep and  stress management and if we lose weight  in the process then great but if we  don't then that doesn't mean that your  blood sugar levels are necessarily in  jeopardy myth number six you are more  likely to develop diabetes if you are a  person of color okay this one is a bit  of a doozy and highly controversial so i  will try my best to be succinct so one  of the most commonly cited risk factors  for diabetes is belonging to a high-risk  ethnic group however we now know that an  increased risk of diabetes is actually  far more complicated than your ethnicity  alone and actually has a lot more to do  with systemic factors affecting minority  groups we call these factors the social  determinants of health and they include  things like income working and living  conditions education physical  environment social support access to  health services as well as racism so  with that in consideration we know that  the socioeconomic disparities affecting  these ethnic groups put them at a  greater risk for not only diabetes  but for other chronic diseases as well  in fact one study found that a low  socioeconomic status was significantly  associated with type 2 diabetes among  these ethnic groups another study found  that belonging to a family of low  socioeconomic status and exposure to  environmental stressors significantly  contributed to the onset of diabetes and  other negative health outcomes among  racialized youth and this is because the  social and environmental factors impact  not only health behaviors but also  health knowledge coping skills and  access to health care moreover we also  know that racial biases and  discrimination can impact our medical  system which can increase patient stress  and hesitancy to access quality health  services so for example one large  prospective study on african american  women found that perceived racism was  strongly associated with a 16 to 31  percent increase in diabetes risk  internalized racism was also found to be  associated with diabetes diagnosis and  elevated fasting glucose furthermore  perceived racial discrimination was  associated with increased diabetes  complications as well as a two-fold  increased risk of developing a chronic  disease and a 20 to 98 percent increase  in all major risk factors so to  summarize that belonging to an ethnic  group and having an increased risk of  developing diabetes is not a you problem  and it has nothing to do with the nature  of your ethnic background instead it's a  complex systemic issue that increases  health disparities in these groups and  puts them at a disadvantage for  developing chronic disease a great  example of this is the healthy immigrant  effect where the health status of  immigrants tend to decline the longer  they remain in their host country due to  environmental factors and lack of access  to health services so to summarize can  you prevent diabetes from progressing  into diabetes yes absolutely you can the  goal is to focus on health behaviors  that not only improve insulin  sensitivity but can also help to lower  stress on the body so this includes  regular physical activity that you  actually enjoy especially aerobic and  resistance exercises and including a  balance of macros that combine carbs  into hunger crushing combos i told you  there was a lot a lot to cover but if  you have questions leave them below and  i can follow up with another video if  you'd like so on that note that is all  that i have for you guys today if you  like this video be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below again  any questions that you have subscribe to  the channel and i'll see you next time  on abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

